    story:"Dr. Aba Turkson is a pharmacist on the infectious disease ward.\n\nA patient with a Klebsiella pneumoniae urinary tract infection is not responding to ceftriaxone. The sensitivity report returns: resistant to all cephalosporins, all penicillins, all fluoroquinolones. Sensitive only to meropenem and colistin.\n\nThe junior doctor looks panicked. \"What do we do?\"\n\nDr. Turkson takes the report calmly. \"This is an ESBL-producing organism. The beta-lactamase the bacteria produces cuts open the beta-lactam ring of all those drugs and inactivates them. Meropenem is a carbapenem — its ring structure resists most ESBLs. We use meropenem.\"\n\n\"And if it was resistant to meropenem too?\"\n\nDr. Turkson pauses. \"Then we would be in the era of pan-resistant organisms. We would use colistin — a drug from the 1950s that we stopped using because of kidney toxicity, now resurrected because we have nothing else.\"\n\nShe looks at the junior doctor.\n\n\"This is why antibiotic stewardship matters. Every unnecessary prescription selects for resistance. Every broad-spectrum drug used when a narrow one would do brings us closer to the day when we have nothing left.\"",
